EXT. FLOODED QUARRY - NIGHT

A bowl of black water ringed by white limestone cliffs. Moonlight lays a long silver road across the surface. Crickets. Distant dogs. Three bicycles lie in the grass.

THEA OYELARAN-MCBRIDE, seventeen, stands on the lip of a twelve-foot ledge in her undershirt and shorts, toes curled over the edge. Below, her cousin JUNIPER "JUNE" BASARAN, sixteen, treads water with her hair plastered to her skull, grinning up. Behind Thea, on the rock, RILEY DESCHAMPS-ADU, seventeen, sits cross-legged with a towel over her shoulders, and she says nothing, and she watches.

JUNE
Jump!

THEA
Give me a second.

JUNE
You've had ten.

THEA
I'm thinking.

JUNE
Don't think. Just fall.

Thea looks down. The water is a black mirror. Her reflection wavers.

RILEY
(from behind)
You don't have to.

THEA
I know I don't.

RILEY
Then why-

THEA
Because June did.

JUNE
I'm right here.

Thea steps back. Steps forward. Back again. The three of them breathe in the dark. A fish rises somewhere and breaks the silver road into pieces.

Thea shouts, a wordless animal sound, and runs three steps and leaps. She flails, arms wheeling, and plunges into the black. The splash soaks June, who yelps and goes under. Riley leaps up, the towel falling, and scrambles to the edge.

RILEY (CONT'D)
Thea!

Nothing. Ripples spread. The moon shatters and slowly reassembles. Then two heads break the surface, gasping, laughing, spitting water, and June and Thea cling to each other in the middle of the quarry.

JUNE
You did it!

THEA
(coughing)
I think I swallowed a frog.

Riley sits down hard on the rock. She presses a hand to her chest, and her shoulders shake, and no one can tell if she is laughing or crying.
